Round a file time before 1970 down to a whole second, not up.
filetime_to_timespec() split the 100-nanosecond count since the epoch
with C division, which truncates toward zero. For a time before the
epoch with a fraction of a second, tv_sec came out one second too high
and tv_nsec negative. stat showed a time of 1969-12-31 23:59:58.25 UTC
as -1 seconds since the epoch, with a fraction of .3544967296, and
touch -r with such a file as the reference failed with "Invalid
argument", since utimensat() rejects a negative tv_nsec.

Borrow a second when tv_nsec is negative, so that tv_nsec always lies
from 0 to 999999999 as POSIX requires.

---
 win32/mingw.c |    5 +++++
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+)

--- a/win32/mingw.c
+++ b/win32/mingw.c
@@ -397,6 +397,11 @@
 
 	ts.tv_sec = (time_t)(winTime / 10000000);
 	ts.tv_nsec = (long)(winTime % 10000000) * 100;
+	if (ts.tv_nsec < 0) {
+		/* round down a time before the epoch */
+		ts.tv_sec--;
+		ts.tv_nsec += 1000000000;
+	}
 
 	return ts;
 }
